//! The analysis that produces a scan's findings from the code resource that was uploaded
//! for it.
//!
//! `GetFindings` "Returns a list of all findings generated by a particular scan" and
//! `Finding` is "Information about a finding that was detected in your code", so the set a
//! scan reports is a FUNCTION OF THE UPLOADED CODE: an archive carrying a detectable
//! weakness produces findings that point at the file, the line and the detector that found
//! it, and an archive carrying none produces an empty list. This module is where that
//! function lives; [`crate::state::Store::apply_run_findings`] is where its output becomes
//! the scan's findings.
//!
//! # What runs, and when
//!
//! 1. The archive is read when it ARRIVES at the published upload target — the one point
//!    the whole of it is in hand — by [`analyse_archive`], which the upload plane calls
//!    outside the store's lock ([`crate::state::UploadedResource::received`]). Reading the
//!    zip container itself is the `zip` crate's job; what this module owns is the BOUNDS
//!    (see below) and the detectors.
//! 2. `CreateScan` copies that analysis onto the run it starts, so what a run reports is
//!    the resource it was STARTED on however the target is written to afterwards.
//! 3. The run's clock-owed transition out of `InProgress` is what makes the result visible:
//!    the findings land in the store then, which is why a reader sees them only once the
//!    run has settled.
//!
//! # The detectors
//!
//! CodeGuru Security's detector library is the service's own; no crate implements it, so
//! the detector set below is this emulator's, written against the weakness classes the
//! library is organised by. Each detector names the CWE class it reports, carries the
//! severity of that class, and is scoped to the languages whose files it applies to — so a
//! finding's `detectorId`, `ruleId`, `type`, `severity` and `remediation.recommendation.url`
//! are all derived from the detector that matched and the language of the file it matched
//! in, never written per finding. The pattern engine is the maintained `regex` crate, and
//! every pattern is a constant of this module compiled ONCE into [`DETECTORS`]: no pattern
//! is ever built from request content.
//!
//! # Bounds
//!
//! The archive is caller content, so every step is bounded before it allocates:
//!
//! * at most [`MAX_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES`] entries of the container are visited and at most
//!   [`MAX_SOURCE_FILES`] of them are read;
//! * one entry is read through a LIMITED reader, so no entry contributes more than
//!   [`MAX_SOURCE_FILE_BYTES`] however its header describes itself (a deflate stream
//!   expands by up to ~1000:1), and every byte INFLATED — including one from an entry that
//!   is then skipped — is charged against [`MAX_SOURCE_BYTES`], so that budget bounds the
//!   decompression work of the whole archive and not only what is kept;
//! * an entry past its bound is skipped rather than truncated, so no file is analysed in
//!   half;
//! * at most [`MAX_LINES_PER_FILE`] lines of one file are examined, and a line contributes
//!   at most [`SNIPPET_LINE_MAX_CHARS`] characters to a snippet;
//! * at most [`FINDINGS_PER_ANALYSIS`] findings are produced, so the work and the output
//!   are both proportional to a constant and not to what was uploaded;
//! * nothing recurses on anything the caller chose: an archive carried INSIDE the archive
//!   is a file like any other and is not opened, so there is no nesting to bound, and the
//!   patterns run on the linear-time `regex` engine rather than a backtracking one.
//!
//! Nothing here opens a file, a socket or a process: the archive is read from the bytes
//! already in memory, and a container this module cannot read produces no findings rather
//! than an error.

use std::collections::BTreeMap;
use std::io::Read;
use std::sync::LazyLock;

use crate::ops::types::{
    CodeLine, FilePath, Finding, Recommendation, Remediation, Resource, Vulnerability,
};

/// The most entries of one container this analysis visits. A zip's entry count comes from
/// its own directory, so it is caller-controlled and capped before anything is read.
pub const MAX_ARCHIVE_ENTRIES: usize = 4_096;

/// The most files of one archive this analysis reads.
pub const MAX_SOURCE_FILES: usize = 64;

/// The most bytes one file of an archive contributes. A file larger than this is SKIPPED
/// rather than cut short, so no file is analysed in half.
pub const MAX_SOURCE_FILE_BYTES: usize = 64 * 1024;

/// The most bytes one archive contributes across all of its files, so what the analysis of
/// one upload holds is bounded whatever was uploaded.
pub const MAX_SOURCE_BYTES: usize = 128 * 1024;

/// The most lines of one file the detectors are run over.
pub const MAX_LINES_PER_FILE: usize = 10_000;

/// The most characters of a matched line a finding's `codeSnippet` carries, so one very
/// long line cannot grow a response without bound.
pub const SNIPPET_LINE_MAX_CHARS: usize = 240;

/// The most findings one archive's analysis produces.
pub const FINDINGS_PER_ANALYSIS: usize = 100;

/// `Finding$generatorId` — "The identifier for the component that generated a finding such
/// as AmazonCodeGuruSecurity".
pub const GENERATOR_ID: &str = "AmazonCodeGuruSecurity";

/// The version suffix a detector identifier carries: the detector library versions each
/// detector, and a finding names the version that found it.
const DETECTOR_VERSION: &str = "v1.0";

/// The classification namespace a finding's `type` is spelled in, the AWS Security Finding
/// Format's `Types` taxonomy, under which a code weakness is reported by its CWE class.
const FINDING_TYPE_NAMESPACE: &str = "Software and Configuration Checks/Vulnerabilities";

/// Where a CWE class is published, which is what a finding's `referenceUrls` points at.
const CWE_REFERENCE_URL_PREFIX: &str = "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/";

/// Where the detector library publishes one detector, which is what a finding's
/// `remediation.recommendation.url` points at.
const DETECTOR_LIBRARY_URL_PREFIX: &str = "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/codeguru/detector-library/";

/// The tag every detector of this set carries, beside its category and its language
/// ("tags or categorizations that are associated with a detector ... defined by type,
/// programming language, or other classification", `Finding$detectorTags`).
const SECURITY_TAG: &str = "security";

/// A language the detector library is organised by: the slug that names it in a detector
/// identifier and in the detector's published URL, and the file extensions this analysis
/// reads as that language.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Language {
    /// `python`, `java`, ... — the slug a detector identifier and a documentation URL
    /// carry.
    pub slug: &'static str,
    /// The extensions, without the dot and in lower case, of the files read as this
    /// language.
    pub extensions: &'static [&'static str],
}

/// The languages this analysis reads. A file whose extension no entry here names is not
/// source this detector set applies to, so it is not analysed at all.
pub const LANGUAGES: &[Language] = &[
    Language {
        slug: "python",
        extensions: &["py"],
    },
    Language {
        slug: "java",
        extensions: &["java"],
    },
    Language {
        slug: "javascript",
        extensions: &["js", "jsx", "mjs", "ts", "tsx"],
    },
    Language {
        slug: "csharp",
        extensions: &["cs"],
    },
    Language {
        slug: "go",
        extensions: &["go"],
    },
    Language {
        slug: "ruby",
        extensions: &["rb"],
    },
];

/// One detector: a weakness class, the CWE it is reported under, how severe an instance of
/// it is, and the pattern that recognises an instance of it in a line of source.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]
pub struct Detector {
    /// The slug that names this detector in an identifier and in its published URL.
    pub slug: &'static str,
    /// `Finding$detectorName` — "The name of the detector that identified the security
    /// vulnerability in your code".
    pub name: &'static str,
    /// The CWE class an instance of this weakness is reported under, as its number.
    pub cwe: u32,
    /// The `Severity` an instance of this weakness carries, one of
    /// `crate::state::SEVERITY_*`.
    pub severity: &'static str,
    /// The categorisation this detector is grouped under, reported as a `detectorTags`
    /// entry.
    pub category: &'static str,
    /// `Finding$description` — "A description of the finding": what the weakness is.
    pub description: &'static str,
    /// `Remediation$recommendation.text` — "The recommended course of action to remediate
    /// the finding".
    pub recommendation: &'static str,
    /// The language slugs this detector applies to.
    pub languages: &'static [&'static str],
    /// The pattern that recognises an instance of this weakness in ONE line of source.
    /// A constant, compiled once into [`DETECTORS`].
    pub pattern: &'static str,
}

/// Every language slug of [`LANGUAGES`], for a detector that applies to all of them.
const EVERY_LANGUAGE: &[&str] = &["python", "java", "javascript", "csharp", "go", "ruby"];

